{
  "id": 78044647,
  "iid": 929,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "Properly set the length field in socket addresses if it's present.",
  "description": "Some UN*Xes (4.4-lite-derived, such as the obscure, little-known macOS,\nFreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD, and DragonFly BSD) have a length field in the\nsocket address structure, and it must be set in order for socket calls\nto work.\n\nCMake already checks whether that field exists.  If it does, set it in\nws_socket_ptoa(), and, in cap_open_socket(), use its value as the\nargument length for connect().  Otherwise, use the length appropriate\nfor the socket address family as the argument length for connect().\n\n(Yes, the second of those is necessary on, at minimum, macOS, and it\nmight also be necessary on other BSD-flavored OSes.)\n\nReport socket() and connect() errors separately, to make it easier to\ndetermine where TCP@ captures fail, if they do fail.",
  "state": "merged",
  "created_at": "2020-11-15T06:52:34.005Z",
  "updated_at": "2020-11-15T07:55:45.427Z",
